.\" LuxOS - manualova stranka lux(1); prikazy musi odpovidat "lux --help" (test_lux_cli.py ji kontroluje)
.TH LUX 1 "2026-11-23" "LuxOS" "LuxOS Manual"
.SH NAME
lux \- LuxOS package manager (repositories, Flathub, community/AUR and .lux apps)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lux
.RI [ options ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B lux
(no command: interactive app store in the terminal)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lux
installs, updates and removes software on LuxOS.
Packages from the LuxOS and Arch Linux repositories are handled directly through libalpm
(the package core is the
.B lux-engine
package); applications from the AUR and Flathub and LuxOS
.B .lux
packages are supported as well.
Commands that change the system ask for the administrator password themselves
(sudo in a terminal, a graphical prompt otherwise), so
.B lux
is normally run without sudo.
.PP
There is no
.B pacman
command on LuxOS. Typing a pacman command (for example
.BR "pacman \-Syu" " or " "lux \-S firefox" )
shows the matching
.B lux
command instead.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B (no command)
Interactive app store in the terminal (fzf): type to search,
.B Enter
installs,
.B Ctrl\-R
removes,
.B Ctrl\-U
updates the whole system and
.B Tab
selects more packages.
.TP
.BR install ", " i " " \fIpkg\fR...
Install packages. Sources are tried in this order: the LuxOS and Arch Linux repositories,
LuxOS
.B .lux
apps, Flathub (for example
.B lux install zoom
installs us.zoom.Zoom), and last the community source (AUR).
Packages that are already installed are skipped. A local file works too:
.I app.lux
or
.IR package.pkg.tar.zst .
See
.B SOURCES
and
.B COMMUNITY PACKAGES (AUR)
below.
.IP
Community packages are build scripts written by other users and are not checked by LuxOS.
Before building, the script is checked by fixed rules and by LuxAI Lite (when you are signed in
to LuxAI). The package is then built and installed into your
.B sandbox
(luxos\-sandbox), isolated from your files and the system.
It goes into the system only with
.B \-\-system
or when you choose it and type the word
.BR system ;
a dangerous script is never installed into the system and an unchecked one never without your
confirmation. LuxAI never installs community packages.
.TP
.BR remove ", " rm " " \fIpkg\fR...
Remove packages and the dependencies nothing else needs. Configuration files you changed stay
as \fIfile\fR.luxsave (with \-n they are deleted too). Flatpak apps (by ID or name) are
removed as well, together with runtimes no app needs any more; their data in ~/.var/app stays.
.TP
.BR update ", " upgrade ", " up
Update everything: the system, community (AUR) packages in the system, Flatpak apps and .lux apps.
New community build scripts are checked again before they are built. Unused Flatpak runtimes are removed.
Configuration steps of other packages run afterwards (/usr/lib/lux/after-update.d).
.TP
.BR check ", " check\-updates
Show available updates without installing them. The package lists are refreshed into a
temporary copy, so this needs no administrator rights and changes nothing on the system.
.TP
.BR search ", " s " [" \-\-installed "] " \fIterm\fR...
Search the repositories, LuxOS apps, Flathub and the community source (AUR); with
.B \-\-installed
only installed packages and apps. A plain word searches names and descriptions. A pattern
searches package names only, case does not matter:
.B ^text
starts with,
.B text$
ends with,
.B ^text$
exact name,
.B *text*
contains;
.B *
is any characters and
.B ?
one character (the whole name must match, e.g.
.BR lib*\-git ),
.B %
is the same as
.B *
(the shell does not expand it). More words or patterns must all match. Quote patterns
(\fBlux search '*qt*'\fR): zsh and other shells expand * and ? themselves. For the AUR a pattern needs
at least 2 letters in a row; ^ and $ are checked after a name search there.
.TP
.BI info " pkg"
Show package details.
.TP
.BR list " [" \-\-installed ]
List installed packages.
.TP
.B list \-\-upgradable
List available updates (from the last downloaded package lists; see
.BR check ).
.TP
.BR "list \-\-lux" ", " "list \-\-aur"
List LuxOS (.lux) or AUR packages.
.TP
.BR "list \-\-explicit" ", " "list \-\-orphans"
Packages you installed on purpose (not as a dependency), or dependencies nothing needs any more
(remove them with
.BR autoremove ).
.TP
.BI reinstall " pkg..."
Install the same version again.
.TP
.B autoremove
Remove dependencies nothing needs any more.
.TP
.BI files " pkg"
Files installed by a package.
.TP
.BR owns ", " owner " " \fIfile\fR
Which package owns a file; a command name from
.B PATH
works too
.RB ( "lux owns firefox" ).
.TP
.B clean
Clean the package cache (keeps the number of versions set by
.BR clean\-keep )
and remove unneeded packages.
.TP
.B fix
Repair a locked package database (only when no package manager is running), delete unfinished
downloads and renew the signing keys (Arch Linux and LuxOS keyrings). Use it when
.B lux
says the database is locked or a signature is unknown or corrupted.
.TP
.B configs
Review and merge new configuration files (.luxnew, .luxsave; older .pacnew, .pacsave) left by updates.
New versions of changed configuration files are normally merged automatically after every update:
values you set stay, new settings and comments are added, and the previous file is kept as
.IR file.lux\-old .
Only a version that cannot be combined without a conflict stays as
.IR file.luxnew ;
the system keeps using your current file.
.TP
.BI config\-diff " file"
Show what the new version of a configuration file changes and choose: combine (keeps your settings),
keep yours, or use the new version (yours is saved as
.IR file.lux\-old ).
.BR \-\-merge ", " \-\-keep ", " \-\-new
choose without asking.
.TP
.BR "config check" ", " "config edit" ", " "config migrate"
Check or edit /etc/lux/lux.conf
.RB ( "config show" " prints the file;"
.B config engine
prints the configuration generated for the core tools (makepkg, hooks);
.B config migrate
converts a file in the old LuxOS 0.9 format to the new one and keeps the original as
.IR lux.conf.old\-format ).
.TP
.BR "config countme" " [" on | off ]
Anonymous weekly count of active LuxOS installs (like Fedora's countme). Without an argument it
shows the exact request: once per calendar week, when the package lists are refreshed as root,
.I https://luxos.lux\-ai.cz/countme
gets only the LuxOS version, architecture, update channel and install age bucket \- no ID and no
personal data.
.B off
turns it off
.RB ( "[general] countme = false" " in /etc/lux/lux.conf)."
.TP
.BR history " [" \fIn\fR "] [" \fIpkg\fR ]
Recent package changes (from /var/log/lux.log).
.TP
.BR pack " " \fIdir\fR " [" \fIout\fR ]
Build a .lux package from a directory with lux.json.
.TP
.B drivers
Detect and install missing drivers.
.TP
.B mirrors
Pick the fastest download mirrors.
.TP
.B doctor
Check system health (services, disk space, configuration, updates).
.TP
.B snapshots
List system snapshots. LuxOS Care takes a read\-only Btrfs snapshot of the system before every
package change (lux, Software Update, Store) and keeps the last 5 plus the last one with the
previous kernel. Each snapshot is in the boot menu as "LuxOS (before update of ...)" (hold Space
while the computer starts); a snapshot started from the menu runs with a temporary writable layer.
.TP
.B snapshot
Take a system snapshot now.
.TP
.BR rollback " [" \fIsnapshot\fR ]
Make a snapshot the system from the next start (asks first). Without a name: the snapshot LuxOS is
running from, otherwise a list to choose from. The current system is kept as a snapshot and
/home is not changed. See
.BR luxos\-care (8)
and "LuxOS Recovery" in the boot menu.
.TP
.BR system " " show | init | edit | check | diff | apply | fix " [" \fIfile\fR ]
The whole system in one LSL file,
.I /etc/luxos/system.lsl
(written by the installer, on older systems by the first update of lux):
packages, drivers, Flathub apps, services, users and groups, computer name, language and formats,
time zone, keyboard and console, network connections (never their passwords), printers, power,
boot menu, LuxOS Care, Lux Guard, firewall, sign-in lockout, kernel parameters and defaults for new users.
.B show
prints a file made from this computer,
.B init
writes it,
.B diff
lists the changes and
.B apply
takes a snapshot, shows the changes, asks and applies them (undo with
.BR "lux rollback" ).
.B fix
repairs drift: it returns the system to the state described in the file (the same as
.BR apply ).
A network connection in use is changed only after a separate question and deleted only with
.BR \-\-force .
Policies of an organization in
.I /etc/luxos/system.d
are merged with the file;
.B show \-\-origin
shows where each value comes from and
.B locks
the settings the organization has locked.
See
.BR luxos\-system (5).
.TP
.BR domain " " discover | join | leave | status | admins | login | set
Organization network: join a Microsoft Active Directory or Samba AD domain, sign in with domain
accounts (also offline), administrators from domain groups. Needs the package
.BR luxos\-domain .
See
.BR luxos\-domain (8).
.SH SOURCES
.B lux install
looks for each name in this order and uses the first source that has it:
.IP 1. 4
the LuxOS and Arch Linux repositories (in the order of the
.RI [repo. NAME ]
tables in
.IR /etc/lux/lux.conf ),
signed and checked before installing;
.IP 2. 4
LuxOS apps
.RB ( .lux
packages, also from the LuxOS Store);
.IP 3. 4
Flathub: the name is looked up through the Flathub API and the download size includes the shared
runtime when it is not installed yet;
.IP 4. 4
the community source (AUR).
.PP
.B \-\-repo
uses only the repositories and
.B \-\-aur
only the community source.
.B lux search
shows where a name is available before you install it.
.SH COMMUNITY PACKAGES (AUR)
The AUR holds build scripts (PKGBUILD) written by other users. LuxOS does not check them, so
.B lux
is careful with them:
.IP \(bu 2
the script is checked by fixed rules and, when you are signed in to LuxAI, by LuxAI Lite;
with
.B review_pkgbuild = true
.RB ( [packages] )
you are also offered the script to read before it is built;
.IP \(bu 2
the package is built on this computer and installed into your sandbox
.RB ( luxos\-sandbox ),
isolated from your files and the system;
.IP \(bu 2
into the system only with
.BR \-\-system ,
or when you choose it and type the word
.BR system .
A script found dangerous is never installed into the system; an unchecked one only after you
confirm it (never with
.BR \-y );
.IP \(bu 2
.B lux update
updates community packages in the system and checks new versions of their scripts again;
.B lux list \-\-aur
lists them.
.PP
.B aur = false
in
.B [packages]
turns the AUR off (for one user in
.IR ~/.config/lux/lux.conf ).
.SH UPDATES
.B lux update
refreshes the package lists, renews the signing keys first and then updates the whole system,
community packages in the system, .lux apps and Flatpak apps (and removes unused Flatpak runtimes).
On a Btrfs system LuxOS Care takes a snapshot before the change, so an update can be undone with
.B lux rollback
or from the boot menu.
.PP
Held packages
.RB ( hold " in " [packages] )
are skipped. A failed download is retried up to 3 times; Ctrl+C during installing finishes the
package in progress and stops safely. Updates in the background (with notifications) are done by
LuxOS Care and Software Update.
.PP
.B Update channel.
.B channel = \(dqstable\(dq
.RB ( [general] )
gets released versions.
.B \(dqstaging\(dq
gets test builds before a release (for testers): addresses starting with
.I https://luxos.lux\-ai.cz/repo/
are read from
.I https://luxos.lux\-ai.cz/staging/
instead. Arch Linux and your own repositories do not change. Switch it in Software Update, with
.B updates.channel
in
.I /etc/luxos/system.lsl
and
.BR "sudo lux system apply" ,
or with
.BR "sudo /usr/lib/lux/lux\-config set\-channel staging" .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Do not ask for confirmation.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-nosave
With
.BR remove :
also delete configuration files you changed. Without it they are kept as
.IR file .luxsave .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Show all details (and raw errors).
.TP
.B \-\-repo
Only the official repositories.
.TP
.BR \-\-aur ", " \-\-community
Only the community source (AUR).
.TP
.B \-\-system
Install a community package into the system instead of the sandbox (explicit permission).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B /etc/lux/lux.conf
holds the repositories, signature checking, held packages and the settings of
.BR lux .
It is a TOML file: tables
.BR [general] ,
.BR [downloads] ,
.BR [security] ,
.BR [packages] ,
.B [paths]
with
.RI \(dq key " = " value \(dq
lines, one
.RI [repo. NAME ]
table per repository and
.B [include]
.RB ( "files = [\(dq/etc/lux/conf.d/*.conf\(dq]" ).
Text values are in quotes, switches are
.B true
or
.BR false ,
lists are
.RB [ \(dqa\(dq ", " \(dqb\(dq ].
Unknown keys are reported as warnings.
The old LuxOS 0.9 format
.RI ( key " " value
lines) is still read;
.B lux config migrate
converts it.
After editing run
.BR "lux config check" ,
or use
.B lux config edit
which checks the file right away.
The full format is described in /usr/share/doc/lux/lux.md and in the LuxOS documentation
(/usr/share/luxos/docs, also on https://luxos.lux\-ai.cz/docs/).
.SS Keys
.TP
.B [general]
.B architecture
(\(dqauto\(dq),
.BR color ", " candy " (Pac\-Man progress bar), " verbose
(true),
.B syslog
(false: also log to the system journal),
.B channel
(\(dqstable\(dq or \(dqstaging\(dq, see
.BR UPDATES ),
.B countme
(true, see
.BR "config countme" ).
.TP
.B [downloads]
.B parallel
(10 files at once),
.B check_space
(true),
.B user
(\(dqalpm\(dq: downloads run without root rights; \(dqnone\(dq),
.B timeout
(true;
.B false
waits for slow mirrors),
.B sandbox
(true: the download process is restricted).
.TP
.B [security]
.B signatures
(\(dqrequired\(dq or \(dqoptional\(dq; \(dqnever\(dq only for a local file:// repository),
.B local_signatures
(\(dqoptional\(dq: packages installed from a file, e.g. built from the AUR),
.B protect
([\(dqlux\-engine\(dq, \(dqglibc\(dq]: packages
.B lux remove
never removes without asking, not even with
.BR \-y ).
.TP
.B [packages]
.B aur
(true),
.B review_pkgbuild
(true),
.B flatpak
(true:
.B lux update
updates Flatpak apps too),
.B cache_keep
(2 versions kept by
.BR "lux clean" ),
.B hold
(packages
.B lux update
skips),
.B ignore_group
(the same for groups),
.B no_upgrade
(paths without the leading /, an update writes a new version as .luxnew instead),
.B no_extract
(path patterns that are never unpacked, e.g. usr/share/doc/*).
.TP
.B [paths]
.BR cache ", " hooks ", " database ", " log ", " keyring
(other locations than the defaults in
.BR FILES ).
.TP
.B [include]
.B files
(more files, read after the whole file; relative paths are relative to the file).
.PP
Lists from all files are added together (a
.B hold
in conf.d adds more packages); other values from a file read later replace earlier ones.
.SS Repositories
One
.RI [repo. NAME ]
table per repository; the order of the tables is the search order. A name with a dot or + needs
quotes:
.RB [repo.\(dq my.repo \(dq].
Keys:
.B server
(\(dqhttps://...\(dq, http, ftp or file, or a list;
.B $repo
and
.B $arch
are filled in),
.B mirrors
(a mirror list file such as \(dq/etc/lux/mirrorlist\(dq),
.B signatures
(overrides the global setting),
.B usage
([\(dqsync\(dq, \(dqsearch\(dq, \(dqinstall\(dq, \(dqupgrade\(dq] or \(dqall\(dq).
.PP
Add a repository in its own file, for example
.IR /etc/lux/conf.d/my.conf :
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[repo.my]
server = "https://example.org/$repo/$arch"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Import its key with
.B "sudo /usr/lib/lux/alpm/bin/lux\-engine\-key \-\-recv\-keys" \fIID\fR
and
.BR \-\-lsign\-key " " \fIID\fR ,
then run
.BR "lux update" .
The
.B multilib
repository (Steam) is turned on with
.BR "sudo /usr/lib/lux/lux\-config enable\-repo multilib" .
.SS Errors
A syntax error or a wrong value is an error with the file name and line number, for example
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
/etc/lux/lux.conf:11: 'parallel' in [downloads] must be a whole
number of at least 1 (without quotes), not "10"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Until it is fixed,
.B lux
(and the Store and LuxOS Care) installs and updates nothing. An unknown key is only a warning in
.B lux config check
(with a suggestion) and is not used.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/lux/lux.conf
Configuration (repositories, signatures, settings).
.TP
.B /etc/lux/conf.d/*.conf
More configuration in the same format.
.TP
.B /etc/lux/mirrorlist
Arch Linux mirrors (lux mirrors).
.TP
.B ~/.config/lux/lux.conf
Per\-user settings (aur, review\-pkgbuild, flatpak, clean\-keep).
.TP
.B /var/lib/lux/
Package database; installed/ holds the .lux packages.
.TP
.B /var/cache/lux/pkg/
Downloaded packages. A damaged file found there is deleted and downloaded again.
.TP
.B /var/log/lux.log
Log of package changes (lux history).
.TP
.B /etc/lux/keyring/
GnuPG keyring for package signatures
.RB ( "lux fix" " repairs it)."
.TP
.B /etc/lux/hooks/
The administrator's own transaction hooks
.RI ( *.hook ,
alpm\-hooks format).
.TP
.BI file .luxnew ", " file .luxsave ", " file .lux\-old
New configuration version that could not be merged, your changed file kept after
.BR remove ,
your previous file kept after an automatic merge.
.TP
.B /run/lux/engine.conf
Generated from lux.conf for the core tools \- do not edit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
No colours and progress bars (also when the output is not a terminal; then it is plain text).
.TP
.B LUX_BACKEND=engine
Use the core tool
.B lux\-engine
instead of libalpm through pyalpm (normally only a fallback when pyalpm cannot be used).
.SH PACMAN EQUIVALENTS
There is no pacman on LuxOS; these
.B lux
commands do the same:
.PP
.TS
l l.
pacman \-Syu	lux update
pacman \-S \fIpkg\fR (\-\-needed)	lux install \fIpkg\fR
pacman \-U \fIfile\fR	lux install \fIfile\fR
pacman \-Rs \fIpkg\fR (\-Rns)	lux remove \fIpkg\fR (\-n)
pacman \-Ss \fIterm\fR	lux search \fIterm\fR
pacman \-Qi / \-Si \fIpkg\fR	lux info \fIpkg\fR
pacman \-Q	lux list
pacman \-Qu / checkupdates	lux list \-\-upgradable / lux check
pacman \-Qe / \-Qm / \-Qdt	lux list \-\-explicit / \-\-aur / \-\-orphans
pacman \-Ql \fIpkg\fR	lux files \fIpkg\fR
pacman \-Qo \fIfile\fR	lux owns \fIfile\fR
paccache \-rk2	lux clean
pacdiff	lux configs
pacman\-key \-\-init/\-\-populate	lux fix
.TE
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.TP
.B The database is locked
Another program is changing packages (Software Update, the Store, LuxOS Care). Wait for it; if
nothing is running any more, run
.BR "lux fix" .
.TP
.B Signature or key errors
.B lux fix
renews the signing keys, then try again.
.TP
.B A downloaded package is corrupted
.B lux
deletes it from /var/cache/lux/pkg and downloads it once more by itself; only when it is still
corrupted does it name the package.
.TP
.B Error in the configuration
.B lux config check
shows the file and line;
.B lux config edit
checks the file right after editing.
.TP
.B An update broke something
.B lux rollback
(or "LuxOS (before update of ...)" in the boot menu) returns the system to the snapshot taken before it.
.TP
.B More details
.B \-v
shows the full package lists, script output and raw libalpm errors;
.B lux doctor
checks services, disk space, the configuration and updates.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (for
.BR check :
the check worked, whether or not updates are available).
.TP
.B 1
Error: the message says what failed and what to do.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B lux update
Update the whole system.
.TP
.B lux check
See what an update would install.
.TP
.B lux install firefox vlc
Install two applications.
.TP
.B lux search \-\-aur spotify
Search only the community source (AUR).
.TP
.B lux search '^python\-*qt*'
Packages whose name starts with python\- and contains qt.
.TP
.B lux search %qt% \-\-installed
Installed packages with qt in the name (% needs no quotes).
.TP
.B lux install zoom
Install Zoom from Flathub.
.TP
.B lux history 50 firefox
The last changes of one package.
.TP
.B lux \-y remove vlc
Remove without asking.
.TP
.B lux install \-\-aur \-\-system IpkgR
Build a package from the AUR and install it into the system instead of the sandbox.
.TP
.B lux install ./app.lux
Install a LuxOS app from a file.
.TP
.B lux owns firefox
Which package the command firefox comes from.
.TP
.B lux list \-\-orphans
Dependencies nothing needs any more (remove them with
.BR "lux autoremove" ).
.TP
.B lux config countme off
Turn off the anonymous weekly install count.
.TP
.B lux rollback
Go back to the system before the last update.
